        <figcaption>US cars and light trucks in use (personal and fleet). 2025 is EIA’s modelled base year; 2026–2040 is EIA’s Annual Energy Outlook 2026 Counterfactual Baseline, a policy-dependent scenario, not a forecast. Heavy trucks and buses are not included.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="ph__why-line"><b>Why it matters for the Powerhouse.</b> Every plug-in vehicle has to stop to charge somewhere. Today that is {mil(fleetNow.ev)} vehicles; by 2040 it could be {mil(fleetEnd.ev)}. Most charge at home, but the more there are, the more stops away from home there will be, and the Powerhouse is a design for those stops.</p>
      <p class="ph__why-line"><b>Constant-ratio illustration.</b> Today {evPerPortUS.toFixed(0)} plug-in vehicles share every public plug. If that holds, holding that cross-year ratio constant would imply about {(portsAtSameRatio2040 / pub.ports).toFixed(0)} times as many public plugs by 2040. This is not a charger-demand forecast; each plug is a stop that can be a car park with a row of posts or a place worth staying in. The map shows where those stops are today.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="comparison" class="ph__essential" aria-labelledby="comparison-title">
